WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.985
Deployment means making a program available for other people to use.

00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:08.097
A script on your laptop works while you run it.

00:00:08.097 --> 00:00:14.851
A shop’s customer needs a service that can receive a message even when your laptop is closed.

00:00:14.851 --> 00:00:17.213
The browser displays the chat.

00:00:17.213 --> 00:00:19.619
A server runs the assistant.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:29.219
An API endpoint is an address on that server that accepts a particular kind of request, such as a message from the chat box.

00:00:29.219 --> 00:00:33.563
Several customers may use the same service at the same time.

00:00:33.563 --> 00:00:36.832
A session identifies a conversation.

00:00:36.832 --> 00:00:43.904
The server must check who can access it before loading its history or returning its information.

00:00:43.904 --> 00:00:47.737
Ana types where is my order on Orbit’s website.

00:00:47.737 --> 00:00:51.613
Her browser sends the message to the shop’s chat address.

00:00:51.613 --> 00:00:56.777
The server receives it and loads the conversation Ana is allowed to use.

00:00:56.777 --> 00:00:59.876
So far the browser is carrying a question.

00:00:59.876 --> 00:01:04.890
It has no need to hold the secret key used to contact the model.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.872
Follow one message from start to finish.

00:01:07.872 --> 00:01:15.596
A customer types into the chat widget on Orbit's website, and the browser sends the message to your API.

00:01:15.596 --> 00:01:22.777
Your API, running on a server, finds that customer's session and hands the message to Scout.

00:01:22.777 --> 00:01:27.677
Scout runs its loop, with the step and spend limits from your guards file.

00:01:27.677 --> 00:01:34.846
It calls tools such as get order and track parcel on the server, and the reply travels back the same way.

00:01:34.846 --> 00:01:41.684
Notice what the browser never sees: the API key, the tools or the system prompt.

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:49.022
Inside the server, the application uses its own private key and runs the usual assistant steps.

00:01:49.022 --> 00:01:54.261
It can ask a tool for Ana’s order and compose a reply from the result.

00:01:54.261 --> 00:01:57.743
Only the permitted reply goes back to the browser.

00:01:57.743 --> 00:02:04.444
Inspect what Ana sees: a message about her parcel, not the server’s private settings.

00:02:04.444 --> 00:02:11.080
Remember that the model has no memory of its own; your code sends the history every time.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:18.314
So the server must know which history belongs to which customer, and that is what the session id is for.

00:02:18.314 --> 00:02:27.788
Picture the conversation ID as a claim ticket. It helps the application find the right history when another message arrives.

00:02:27.788 --> 00:02:35.695
The application keeps each history separate and checks the customer is allowed to access the requested order.

00:02:35.695 --> 00:02:45.260
A Python dictionary works on one server, but it is lost on every restart, so real deployments keep sessions in a database.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:49.956
Imagine two customers type into the site at the same instant.

00:02:49.956 --> 00:02:52.692
Ana sends her order number first.

00:02:52.692 --> 00:03:00.299
On the server, the program looks up the conversation that belongs to her session before adding that new message.

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:04.396
Now follow Owen's message down the same network path.

00:03:04.396 --> 00:03:09.796
The server code can be shared, but the history loaded for Owen must be different.

00:03:09.796 --> 00:03:17.150
The session identifies a conversation; access checks still have to protect the underlying order.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.798
Open two separate test sessions.

00:03:19.798 --> 00:03:24.770
Give each a different pretend order and ask the same follow-up in both.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:28.200
Compare what each window shows with its own record.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:34.088
This catches mix-ups that a successful single-window demo would never reveal.

00:03:34.088 --> 00:03:39.511
Now open two test conversations, one for Ana and another for Owen.

00:03:39.511 --> 00:03:44.248
Give them different order numbers and alternate their follow-up questions.

00:03:44.248 --> 00:03:47.442
Each reply should refer to its own order.

00:03:47.442 --> 00:03:51.649
One successful message tests that the service is reachable.

00:03:51.649 --> 00:03:59.414
Two separate conversations test something just as important: whose information each reply uses.
